("The world owes about $348 trillion: governments about 107, companies about 100, banks 76, households about 65; about $43,000 per person.","The world owes about $348","IIF1: IIF Global Debt Monitor, end-2025 $348.3T; government $106.7T, non-financial corporate $100.6T, households $64.6T; financial sector by subtraction about $76T. K02: 348T / 8.1B = $42,963 per person. Correct.",['IIF1']),
("Counting a dollar a second, a million takes 11 days, a billion 31 years, a trillion 31,700 years.","A trillion takes 31,700","K02: 11.6 days, 31.7 years, 31,688 years. Reproduces.",[]),
("Measured against the world economy, global debt has fallen five years running.","global debt has actually fallen","IIF1: global debt-to-GDP fell for a fifth straight year to about 308% in 2025. Correct.",['IIF1']),
("The Bank of England (2014) explained that bank loans create deposits; about 97% of money is bank deposits.","In 2014, the Bank of England","Correct by analyst recall: \"Money creation in the modern economy\", BoE Quarterly Bulletin 2014 Q1; the 97% UK broad-money share matches that period. The caveats (capital rules, demand) are fair.",[]),
